Z||000009||ChadMan||07-19-2000||12:36 PM||||A 300hp Fiero would definitely outrun a police car.  I'd venture to say a stock V6 Fiero with a manual transmission would outrun most police cars, one on one.<P>Except that the police have radios, stop sticks, and lots and lots of buddies.  And if the chopper comes in, forget it - you're not going to outrun them no matter what you're driving.  Smokey and the Bandit was cool to watch, but in real, Burt Reynolds would have his skull thumped by a few batons to the head and been charged with felony fleeing and eluding.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||07-19-2000||01:23 PM||||What Chad said, unless you've lowered your Fiero.  Then you could easily elude the copter.  Just drive under a semi trailer.  How's he gonna see you then?||||reg||1
Z||000011||DJRice||07-19-2000||02:11 PM||||I dont think that a stock v6 fiero will outrun a Police Interceptor...You might jump it off the line, but all of the motors in those cars are tuned for more power.  Probably up to 300HP at least.  And will all the torque those motors pump, you might not even jump it off the line.  Ford and Chevy dont go through the trouble of making a "police interceptor" badge just to sell them to police departments, they "tune" the engines and beef up the suspension and brakes.  <P>For instance, the Chevy Caprice Interceptors had the tuned up LT1 motors.  The 93-96 Impala SS was basically a Police Interceptor with leather seats and a cool trim package for the general public.  Run one of those cars against a Fiero, and eventually the displacement difference in the engine will push that Chevy faster than a stock fiero will go.<P>BTW, anyone who saw "Gone in sixty seconds" knows that even the police chopper does not have a chance when you have a 67 Shelby GT500 with Nitrous!<P>------------------<BR>Dillon - Titusville, FL<BR>Black '87 SE V6<BR>"Matched Perfect and Staggered Special"||||reg||1
Z||000012||SpeedPhreak||07-19-2000||03:47 PM||||my stock 74 nova w/a 250 straight six beat a cop.. but i had a big jump on him & got off the hgwy & took a bunch of side streets.<P>my 95hp crotch rocket gets away everytime it tries  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (not that ive ever been the pilot  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">).<P>i would hate to see a new c5 as a cop car though.. a cop in my town has one w/a little work (exhaust, intake, etc..) & he said the speedo hit 190 b4 he ran out of room... dont think u would get away from that if it was a cop car.||||reg||1
Z||000013||ChadMan||07-19-2000||03:57 PM||||Hmm...yes, Chevy did offer the LT1 as an option with the police package, and I think Ford offered the DOHC 4.6 from the Mark VIII as an option with their Crown Vic police package.  These cars would give the V6 Fiero a run for its money, no doubt - but I wonder how many police departments opted for the hot motors?  They're usually pretty budget-conscious (check out the cheapa$$ tires on many police cars).<P>Columbus, OH's older cruisers are Caprices with the throttle-body 305 (190 hp) and Crown Vics with the SOHC 4.6 (something like 210 hp).  And that light bar creates a lot of drag.  My money would be on the Fiero over either of these.  (I like freedom, so I'm not going to test out my theory.)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||Pontiaddict||07-19-2000||05:41 PM||||A cop car could beat a Fiero if they both started at the same time and let top speed be the determining factor. That extra half ton of weight could do some damage too.<P>Of course the local cops around here drive front drivers anyway.<P>It's the county and state boys you gotta worry about.||||reg||1
